Welcome to Unit One!

You have already learned some words, including some **verbs**. Verbs are words of actions, such as "to walk", "to drink", "to reside", i.e. words that you put "to" in front of. In Finnish, there is no word for "to". Instead, the base verb is only one word, like: to walk = *kävellä*, to drink = *juoda*, to speak = *puhua*. What you might notice is that these words, while being very different from each other, all end in a vowel, either *a* or *ä*. Unlike in English, all verbs are modified differently when they refer to a person. Modifying verbs is called "conjugation" and will be explained next.


^Conjugation of verbs^

All verbs have a "default" form (the infinitive), like *kävellä*, *juoda*, *syödä* etc.

In English, conjugation works like this: "to eat" becomes "I eat", "you eat", but "he eat*s*"

In Finnish, a verb is modified **in all persons**, like in the following example:

to eat = syödä
I eat = syön
you eat = syöt
he eats = hän syö
we eat = syömme
you (plural) eat = syötte
they eat = he syövät

*minä, sinä, me, te (I, you, we, you) can be left out if you want, because the ending already indicates which person it is, but *hän* and *he* (he/she and they) is always necessary to include.

What we notice here is that the end of the infinitive *syödä* (-dä) falls off, making the stem "syö" and a new ending is then added to this stem (*n* for "I", *t* for "you" etc.).

However, the word *juosta* (to run) is conjugated differently:

to run = juosta (stem = *juo-*)
I run = juoksen
you run = juokset
he runs = hän juoksee
we run = juoksemme
you (plural) run = juoksette
they run = he juoksevat

This word does get the same endings as the previous word, but here even the stem changes, e.g. *juosta* got a "k" while *syödä* didn't. This is because these two verbs belong to different categories, and all seven verb categories receive different stem changes, but the endings are always the same. It is best to learn verb conjugations in sentences rather than mechanically in lists like in the above examples, but if you want to, you can screenshot the tables below for a quick reference of examples from all the verb categories. That being said, using a separate learning material, like a Finnish language textbook to use alongside this course, is always recommended.
